1
00:00:01,961 --> 00:00:17,690
[SPEAKER_01]: That's one thing that people would really struggle to understand when we say, like, the... in order to enter the first jhāna, you factually have to give up even the liability to the five hindrances, not just, like, specific manifestations.

2
00:00:17,710 --> 00:00:24,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then it would sound like, also, you're saying you have to basically become an arahant in order to enter the first jhāna.

3
00:00:25,294 --> 00:00:25,895
[SPEAKER_01]: But the thing is,

4
00:00:27,465 --> 00:00:32,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the liability to the hindrances is factually gone at that time, whether you like it or not.

5
00:00:33,088 --> 00:00:36,710
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean that they will not come back.

6
00:00:40,232 --> 00:00:53,320
[SPEAKER_05]: Because if you incline the mind, if you give in to the things that, at that time, don't feel like they're a problem, the hindrances will return.

7
00:01:14,547 --> 00:01:36,372
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I thought we could discuss mindfulness from the perspective of losing mindfulness, let's say, because we already talked in the past about the four satipaṭṭhānas, and to some extent we discussed the hindrances and things like that, but it was more on a broader level.

8
00:01:37,152 --> 00:01:44,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I think, first of all, we could start by defining what is mindfulness,

9
00:01:44,730 --> 00:01:51,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when many people hear that word, they might confuse it with a moment-to-moment focused awareness.

10
00:01:52,694 --> 00:01:58,416
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that very reason, they wouldn't necessarily realize how they lose it.

11
00:01:58,777 --> 00:02:10,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they would think that, you know, if I just... if I can just focus more, focus harder, not have any external circumstances,

12
00:02:11,190 --> 00:02:13,212
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can we define mindfulness in a more accurate way?

13
00:02:31,597 --> 00:02:40,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, not necessarily focusing on the angle of mindfulness, you could also call it samādhi or composure, because it goes hand in hand.

14
00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:47,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Solidified mindfulness is basically the same as composure being established.

15
00:02:49,482 --> 00:02:57,904
[SPEAKER_01]: So, experientially, how does it feel to have mindfulness established, let's say?

16
00:03:20,741 --> 00:03:22,041
[SPEAKER_03]: Still, calm.

17
00:03:26,469 --> 00:03:28,150
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a result of the mindfulness.

18
00:03:28,530 --> 00:03:35,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can be still or calm, actually, due to lack of mindfulness as well.

19
00:03:35,293 --> 00:03:35,533
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

20
00:03:36,974 --> 00:03:38,894
[SPEAKER_02]: Having a perspective upon the situation.

21
00:03:39,094 --> 00:03:39,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

22
00:03:40,235 --> 00:03:41,716
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be one way.

23
00:03:41,796 --> 00:03:46,918
[SPEAKER_00]: But is that perspective something that you are kind of actively maintaining?

24
00:03:49,321 --> 00:03:57,813
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the right mindfulness is based on understanding what is already there, given.

25
00:03:59,195 --> 00:04:04,081
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, the mindfulness is the recollection or the remembering of

26
00:04:04,842 --> 00:04:08,745
[SPEAKER_02]: Those aspects of your experience that have to be there already.

27
00:04:10,006 --> 00:04:13,048
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, those things you don't need to maintain.

28
00:04:15,269 --> 00:04:27,938
[SPEAKER_02]: But, then the question is, or I think this is what you're asking, like, so one can recollect those things which are like satipaṭṭhāna, or death, or asupa, or any context that you set up,

29
00:04:29,025 --> 00:04:33,648
[SPEAKER_02]: That is the direction of the right mindfulness, but then that context or the perspective can be lost.

30
00:04:34,688 --> 00:04:46,315
[SPEAKER_02]: So, we're working towards finding out what are the factors that one falls away from that context, but those things that you were established in, in and of themselves, they don't go anywhere.

31
00:04:46,415 --> 00:04:57,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but at the same time, the mindfulness itself, the perspective over those things, is it something you do or is it something that... or is it not something you do?

32
00:04:59,504 --> 00:05:02,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's put it this way.

33
00:05:02,927 --> 00:05:04,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you just say that it's already there?

34
00:05:05,850 --> 00:05:12,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you just say it like that, then it sounds like you don't have to do anything and we're already enlightened or something.

35
00:05:12,136 --> 00:05:13,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

36
00:05:14,158 --> 00:05:15,479
[SPEAKER_02]: That's clearly not the meaning here.

37
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:19,363
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, so you do need to understand

38
00:05:21,137 --> 00:05:28,359
[SPEAKER_02]: Those aspects or principles to begin with, because otherwise you can't correctly recollect them.

39
00:05:29,599 --> 00:05:31,419
[SPEAKER_02]: And that won't be the right perspective.

40
00:05:32,299 --> 00:05:35,500
[SPEAKER_02]: And you do, in a sense, need to set it up.

41
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:43,962
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you do that properly, then, in a sense, your mindfulness will be there on its own.

42
00:05:44,582 --> 00:05:46,182
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not going to be like that from the beginning.

43
00:05:46,743 --> 00:05:48,403
[SPEAKER_02]: So there is an aspect of understanding,

44
00:05:48,992 --> 00:05:50,534
[SPEAKER_02]: And there is an aspect of establishing.

45
00:05:50,875 --> 00:05:53,919
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you have to just let it endure on its own.

46
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:57,364
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, the question is, how are you going to find out once it's been lost?

47
00:05:57,825 --> 00:05:59,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but...

48
00:05:59,046 --> 00:06:02,892
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point I'm trying to highlight is that, when mindfulness is established,

49
00:06:04,453 --> 00:06:08,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's important to recognize it's not something you do.

50
00:06:09,036 --> 00:06:12,518
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to work to clarify it and understand it, as you said.

51
00:06:13,099 --> 00:06:18,822
[SPEAKER_00]: But once it's there, it's kind of there on its own.

52
00:06:18,902 --> 00:06:20,604
[SPEAKER_00]: It's containing you, in a way.

53
00:06:20,724 --> 00:06:21,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

54
00:06:21,664 --> 00:06:23,505
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not something that you are doing.

55
00:06:24,246 --> 00:06:30,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is actually precisely one of the reasons why, if you're not careful, you will lose it.

56
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:36,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it feels like you don't need to maintain it anymore.

57
00:06:36,342 --> 00:06:54,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Precisely because of that, factual fact, in a sense, you don't need to maintain it anymore, but you overlook that there are things that you could do that will slowly degrade what you've built up.

58
00:06:55,473 --> 00:06:57,454
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you said it's a perspective, but

59
00:06:58,786 --> 00:07:00,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it a perspective of something specific?

60
00:07:01,269 --> 00:07:05,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it like a specific thought that endures in your mind?

61
00:07:06,114 --> 00:07:08,877
[SPEAKER_02]: Or is it something else?

62
00:07:10,589 --> 00:07:18,113
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the perspective is of the totality, quote-unquote, of this experience.

63
00:07:18,933 --> 00:07:21,695
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that is part of the understanding, right?

64
00:07:21,715 --> 00:07:29,278
[SPEAKER_02]: So, as we discussed with the satipaṭṭhāna, that there are four, let's say, phenomenological totalities.

65
00:07:29,679 --> 00:07:36,642
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they all... they're slightly different vantage points, but they disclose your experience as a whole.

66
00:07:37,222 --> 00:07:39,904
[SPEAKER_02]: That is... that would be the right mindfulness.

67
00:07:40,663 --> 00:07:48,887
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's not like that, and you're going to focus on something specific, it will always be, as we said many times, in front of you.

68
00:07:49,367 --> 00:07:54,409
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you try to focus on that very hard, and then the whole question of distraction comes in.

69
00:07:54,429 --> 00:08:03,794
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you know, there's going to be a conflict between looking at that glass and a particular thought arising, and I feel I need to suppress it because I want to focus on the thing there.

70
00:08:04,174 --> 00:08:08,756
[SPEAKER_02]: And then all of that comes along, which actually has nothing really to do with what we're talking about now.

71
00:08:09,881 --> 00:08:19,888
[SPEAKER_02]: So, assuming that that wrong direction is out of the way, then the mindfulness is about understanding this situation as a whole.

72
00:08:20,208 --> 00:08:26,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but is there a specific... is it that the mindfulness has a specific theme?

73
00:08:27,702 --> 00:08:32,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it tied to it, or is it something that's not linked to a specific theme?

74
00:08:32,584 --> 00:08:41,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you choose to develop mindfulness of feelings, is it that just feelings is what comes up for your perspective only?

75
00:08:41,528 --> 00:08:45,149
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if you want to be mindful of something else, you have to change your object?

76
00:08:46,410 --> 00:08:47,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that how it works, or...?

77
00:08:49,958 --> 00:08:55,883
[SPEAKER_02]: If you have discerned feeling on that most general level, then your mindfulness can be there.

78
00:08:56,224 --> 00:09:06,573
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, there will be mindfulness of feeling, and then, within that, all sorts of other things can take place, but that will not be in conflict with being mindful of that general feeling.

79
00:09:07,054 --> 00:09:16,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, so what I'm pointing at is, mindfulness is a kind of a... it's a quality in and of itself.

80
00:09:17,721 --> 00:09:21,783
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not defined by... it's just a broadening of perspective.

81
00:09:22,003 --> 00:09:24,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness, you could say, is the broadening of perspective.

82
00:09:24,704 --> 00:09:40,151
[SPEAKER_00]: And it doesn't matter whether that broadening of perspective is kind of instantiated through perspective of the body, perspective of feelings, perspective of mind, perspective of phenomena.

83
00:09:40,411 --> 00:09:43,913
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have developed that mindfulness properly, on the right level,

84
00:09:44,853 --> 00:09:52,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Any of these four things you could take up for reflection with the same mindfulness that you've built up.

85
00:09:52,276 --> 00:10:02,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, it's just like a space, let's say, a space that opens up and then that space can be filled in with any, any, basically anything you, that takes your fantasy.

86
00:10:03,485 --> 00:10:12,028
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's another... the reason why I'm bringing that up is because that dispels the whole notion of it being about locking your mind into one specific thing.

87
00:10:12,448 --> 00:10:23,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can think of it as, on a very wrong, extremely wrong level, of thinking of it as a specific object of concentrated focus.

88
00:10:23,991 --> 00:10:29,093
[SPEAKER_00]: But even if you start thinking about it as a context or a theme or a reflection,

89
00:10:30,033 --> 00:10:40,440
[SPEAKER_00]: You might also still have some lingering notion that it's about, like, maintain... like, keeping those... keeping your mind within that specific topic.

90
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:43,062
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not necessarily the case.

91
00:10:43,102 --> 00:10:48,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the mindfulness is just the ability... it's just the perspective, let's say.

92
00:10:48,946 --> 00:10:53,128
[SPEAKER_00]: The ability to see your experience for what it is at any given time.

93
00:10:53,409 --> 00:10:55,050
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a sense of clarity.

94
00:10:56,090 --> 00:10:58,412
[SPEAKER_00]: And... so...

95
00:11:00,035 --> 00:11:05,077
[SPEAKER_05]: Having said that, then, how is it that you will lose it?

96
00:11:05,157 --> 00:11:15,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it's not about keeping it tied to the same theme, even, it's not that I'll need to be thinking about feelings the whole evening in order to develop mindfulness.

97
00:11:16,081 --> 00:11:20,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Like I said, it's a direction, so you could cultivate it in multiple different ways.

98
00:11:20,763 --> 00:11:28,386
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how is it that you will lose it, given that it's not tied to a specific thing?

99
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:35,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Not tied to a specific thing, but clearly, it does get lost.

100
00:11:35,983 --> 00:11:43,087
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that it's just like, once you establish mindfulness properly, that's it, you're done and become an Arhat.

101
00:11:52,092 --> 00:11:52,932
[SPEAKER_03]: Any ideas, anyone?

102
00:11:55,554 --> 00:11:57,555
[SPEAKER_03]: By accepting the light and

103
00:12:00,771 --> 00:12:10,598
[SPEAKER_03]: Entertaining that relief that other sensuality or, let's say, doubt, for example, presents you with.

104
00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:27,232
[SPEAKER_03]: So, generally, it would begin with, like, subtler things or, like, minor things, but then each time you kind of prioritize that relief, you are basically

105
00:12:29,765 --> 00:12:34,348
[SPEAKER_01]: And why is it... why does it present itself as relief?

106
00:12:34,808 --> 00:12:41,752
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're kind of already clear about the fact that that's not where relief comes from, why?

107
00:12:42,073 --> 00:12:43,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you still give in?

108
00:12:45,575 --> 00:12:47,216
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mindfulness... it's clear.

109
00:12:47,536 --> 00:12:57,562
[SPEAKER_01]: I know that this is... to a certain extent, you might already be experiencing the right type of relief, of giving up, of perspective, of dispassion.

110
00:13:01,957 --> 00:13:04,158
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is it still... why would you still accept it?

111
00:13:04,178 --> 00:13:04,678
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

112
00:13:05,538 --> 00:13:08,119
[SPEAKER_03]: Because of the habit, basically.

113
00:13:08,139 --> 00:13:13,781
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay, yeah, that's on a broader scale, but at that moment, like, what is it that makes you... Oh, pain.

114
00:13:14,702 --> 00:13:15,122
[SPEAKER_03]: Discomfort.

115
00:13:15,802 --> 00:13:16,082
[SPEAKER_05]: Uh-huh.

116
00:13:17,403 --> 00:13:26,566
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're seeing that discomfort as not yours, as something that arose on its own because your mindfulness is established, then why do you... why would you still do that?

117
00:13:28,067 --> 00:13:28,647
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't think you would.

118
00:13:30,113 --> 00:13:44,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but what I'm saying, you could have the clarity that, oh, this is arisen on its own, this is not mine, this is impermanent, and all this stuff, and yet you would give it.

119
00:13:46,938 --> 00:13:51,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you wouldn't see the danger?

120
00:13:52,260 --> 00:13:52,880
[SPEAKER_05]: Sure, but...

121
00:13:55,052 --> 00:14:00,395
[SPEAKER_05]: Like if you... the thing is, like we said before, the mindfulness is not even something that you actively do.

122
00:14:01,075 --> 00:14:11,040
[SPEAKER_05]: So, in a way, the clarity that it is dangerous, that it is impermanent, that it is suffering, that it is not yours, that clarity would be enduring there even, like, regardless of what you do.

123
00:14:11,500 --> 00:14:19,729
[SPEAKER_05]: Even if you don't actively think about, oh, this is dangerous, you know that it's dangerous because the mindfulness is established on that peripheral level.

124
00:14:20,409 --> 00:14:27,957
[SPEAKER_05]: So, given that that's... despite the fact that's there, you would give in.

125
00:14:28,298 --> 00:14:28,498
[SPEAKER_05]: What?

126
00:14:40,867 --> 00:14:42,888
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

127
00:14:43,008 --> 00:14:44,469
[SPEAKER_05]: That's as close as you can get.

128
00:14:44,509 --> 00:14:46,091
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just carelessness.

129
00:14:46,471 --> 00:14:51,654
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not that you don't know that it's not the right direction.

130
00:14:51,875 --> 00:14:53,256
[SPEAKER_05]: You know that it is.

131
00:14:53,916 --> 00:14:57,719
[SPEAKER_05]: But the carelessness is, in a way,

132
00:15:00,555 --> 00:15:03,916
[SPEAKER_05]: By putting the wrong emphasis on like... because, see, you could take it either way.

133
00:15:04,697 --> 00:15:19,143
[SPEAKER_05]: You take it as... when you have that clarity and it feels like, you know, everything is... you see things as they are, as Buddha would say, it can feel as if, okay, so it doesn't matter what I do then, because this clarity is going to be there anyway.

134
00:15:20,656 --> 00:15:22,637
[SPEAKER_05]: But you could take that in two directions.

135
00:15:22,797 --> 00:15:23,898
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't matter what I do.

136
00:15:24,958 --> 00:15:28,380
[SPEAKER_05]: Therefore, I would break my restraint and give in to the hindrances.

137
00:15:28,840 --> 00:15:32,341
[SPEAKER_05]: And factually, the clarity will not fade away immediately.

138
00:15:33,122 --> 00:15:35,703
[SPEAKER_05]: Because it kind of precedes the domain of action.

139
00:15:37,365 --> 00:15:41,969
[SPEAKER_05]: Or you could take it the other way and say, well, it doesn't matter what I do, so let me not do it.

140
00:15:42,009 --> 00:15:44,332
[SPEAKER_05]: Let me not give in.

141
00:15:45,513 --> 00:15:52,800
[SPEAKER_05]: But the thing is, it's like, the clarity, the domain of the mind in general, it's not something... it's always indirect.

142
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:57,484
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not something that you can kind of measure, like, this is how much mindfulness I have.

143
00:15:58,025 --> 00:15:59,967
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what you would do is, like, you would give in,

144
00:16:00,863 --> 00:16:03,444
[SPEAKER_05]: And, eh... you kind of check, okay, it's...

145
00:16:03,464 --> 00:16:04,184
[SPEAKER_05]: I haven't lost it.

146
00:16:04,264 --> 00:16:04,845
[SPEAKER_05]: It's still there.

147
00:16:05,065 --> 00:16:05,705
[SPEAKER_05]: It's still there.

148
00:16:05,925 --> 00:16:07,626
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you feel like, okay, well, it doesn't matter.

149
00:16:07,666 --> 00:16:09,687
[SPEAKER_05]: Let me just fully go wild.

150
00:16:10,447 --> 00:16:14,729
[SPEAKER_05]: And, eh... and later on, you would look back and you realize, oh, wait, it's gone.

151
00:16:15,669 --> 00:16:17,530
[SPEAKER_05]: Abruptly, you just realize, oh, wait, it's gone.

152
00:16:17,790 --> 00:16:21,471
[SPEAKER_05]: You didn't even see, eh... there was no warning sign.

153
00:16:21,531 --> 00:16:22,112
[SPEAKER_05]: There was nothing.

154
00:16:22,392 --> 00:16:27,814
[SPEAKER_05]: In the same way that there was no warning sign when you were developing it properly, you suddenly realized, oh, wait,

155
00:16:28,354 --> 00:16:32,275
[SPEAKER_05]: I've been, I've been, like, as that simile says, like, wearing down the axe handle.

156
00:16:32,795 --> 00:16:41,098
[SPEAKER_05]: I've been wearing it down, and suddenly you realize, oh, now I cannot even forget that things are impermanent, that the body comes first, all these things.

157
00:16:41,118 --> 00:16:42,838
[SPEAKER_05]: So, okay, now mindfulness is established.

158
00:16:43,859 --> 00:16:47,059
[SPEAKER_05]: So, so, yeah, it works both ways.

159
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:51,521
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's why it's, it's important to, to, to be clear that it's that

160
00:16:52,450 --> 00:17:07,707
[SPEAKER_05]: It's a dimension that doesn't depend on you, because then, first, that would allow you to develop it properly, and secondly, it would also allow you to protect it, because if you're still putting the emphasis on, like,

161
00:17:09,555 --> 00:17:30,044
[SPEAKER_05]: Your kind of aspect of the equation, you forget that actually there's... like, you may not feel any problem with doing these things that go against the direction that you've developed, but there is that kind of a delayed effect that these things will lead to, and it always snowballs.

162
00:17:30,084 --> 00:17:35,046
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the more you give in, the kind of easier it gets to continue in that direction.

163
00:17:35,887 --> 00:17:36,207
[SPEAKER_05]: So...

164
00:17:42,882 --> 00:17:45,244
[SPEAKER_05]: And what is it that you would give in to, specifically?

165
00:17:46,245 --> 00:17:50,148
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, let's say, somebody... you might not even break the precepts.

166
00:17:51,929 --> 00:17:58,775
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be assumed that, you know, if you even got to the point where your mindfulness was established, you were already established in virtue.

167
00:17:59,475 --> 00:18:06,261
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is it that, despite not breaking the precepts, you would still lose your mindfulness?

168
00:18:06,301 --> 00:18:08,462
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it has to start with mental action, right?

169
00:18:09,875 --> 00:18:17,161
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you're not gonna act out by body unless you've been welcoming thoughts related to acting out by body.

170
00:18:18,722 --> 00:18:22,685
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's more subtle than breaking the precepts.

171
00:18:23,746 --> 00:18:31,172
[SPEAKER_04]: And you can almost, like, not to say that it's, like, accidental, but... Well, I guess, if you are mindful, you can't overlook it.

172
00:18:31,692 --> 00:18:39,298
[SPEAKER_04]: If you are partly mindful, you won't overlook it, but kind of like you say, you can just kind of see it as, oh, well, you know,

173
00:18:40,812 --> 00:18:42,532
[SPEAKER_04]: This is insignificant.

174
00:18:42,672 --> 00:18:43,473
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another point.

175
00:18:43,553 --> 00:18:44,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it accidental?

176
00:18:44,513 --> 00:19:05,878
[SPEAKER_05]: Because I think there's often this perception that, um, you know, people are... do kind of get a sense of, oh, yeah, I've developed this clarity because I've been doing this 10-day retreat, and then they would still have the sense that when they go back, it's kind of like, because of the external world and because of the situations that they have to face,

177
00:19:07,381 --> 00:19:11,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Those external things are making them lose their mindfulness accidentally.

178
00:19:12,844 --> 00:19:33,917
[SPEAKER_05]: So, eh... if it's not accidental, as you said, it's related to mental action, but eh... the important thing to see is that, like, that mental action isn't just... like, it's... it's...

179
00:19:35,162 --> 00:19:40,144
[SPEAKER_05]: It's a simple failure to protect the same thing that you were already doing.

180
00:19:41,004 --> 00:19:45,566
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not even like some secret information that you didn't have.

181
00:19:45,786 --> 00:19:53,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, unfortunately, they told you how to build up the mindfulness, but nobody taught you how to maintain it, as if it's a different thing.

182
00:19:54,670 --> 00:19:58,551
[SPEAKER_05]: It's literally the same context that you've been maintaining.

183
00:19:59,231 --> 00:19:59,952
[SPEAKER_05]: It's still there.

184
00:20:01,047 --> 00:20:08,852
[SPEAKER_05]: But you, like we said before, carelessly choose to just emphasize the other stuff.

185
00:20:09,513 --> 00:20:18,058
[SPEAKER_05]: So, but what are the things that would take you in that opposite direction, let's say?

186
00:20:24,002 --> 00:20:27,465
[SPEAKER_03]: Detection, company, work.

187
00:20:29,148 --> 00:20:29,668
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, okay.

188
00:20:29,728 --> 00:20:31,008
[SPEAKER_05]: Those are kind of the external things.

189
00:20:31,048 --> 00:20:37,290
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as we said, mentally, even before you give in by body and speech.

190
00:20:39,850 --> 00:20:40,430
[SPEAKER_01]: He nurses.

191
00:20:42,431 --> 00:20:42,931
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, exactly.

192
00:20:42,971 --> 00:20:49,732
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, as you read in the suttas, it's like, they say, weakeners of wisdom.

193
00:20:50,633 --> 00:20:54,273
[SPEAKER_05]: Or, like, they constrict the mind.

194
00:20:55,094 --> 00:20:56,474
[SPEAKER_05]: And that constriction is

195
00:20:57,780 --> 00:21:05,085
[SPEAKER_05]: 100% exactly the opposite direction of the broader perspective that mindfulness is.

196
00:21:06,126 --> 00:21:13,030
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you don't even have... that's exactly why, even without breaking the precepts, you would still be able to

197
00:21:16,087 --> 00:21:22,376
[SPEAKER_05]: Emphasize the wrong direction and pretty much willingly wear away your mindfulness.

198
00:21:22,416 --> 00:21:28,364
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, yeah, restlessness, doubt, and let's say you develop some clarity about

199
00:21:29,668 --> 00:21:30,669
[SPEAKER_05]: Some aspect of dhamma.

200
00:21:31,470 --> 00:21:39,477
[SPEAKER_05]: And you saw that, oh, what the dhamma, the practice, is to not act out of painful feelings.

201
00:21:40,477 --> 00:21:43,840
[SPEAKER_05]: But before, that was just theory, that was just information.

202
00:21:43,880 --> 00:21:46,483
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, you're seeing it on that experiential level.

203
00:21:46,563 --> 00:21:48,004
[SPEAKER_05]: You have that clarity developed.

204
00:21:50,685 --> 00:22:13,719
[SPEAKER_05]: But then, doubts come up, questions come up, about things that you know are secondary, things that you know are... that you know that what matters is not acting out of unpleasant feelings, and you know that any type of actually relevant wisdom would come out of that, wouldn't come out of you clarifying abstract ideas.

205
00:22:14,679 --> 00:22:15,340
[SPEAKER_05]: But still,

206
00:22:16,844 --> 00:22:23,272
[SPEAKER_05]: The pressure is just too much for you to stick to what you already know is right.

207
00:22:23,813 --> 00:22:27,177
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's basically that.

208
00:22:30,850 --> 00:22:37,594
[SPEAKER_05]: A simple choice to not protect the same thing that was already there.

209
00:22:37,794 --> 00:22:40,856
[SPEAKER_05]: And, like you were saying before, you kind of know.

210
00:22:41,036 --> 00:22:48,161
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, if somebody were to just suddenly, I don't know, break into your room and ask you a question at that moment, which is a wholesome thing to do?

211
00:22:49,922 --> 00:22:55,465
[SPEAKER_05]: To go through with that doubt or to see that as a painful feeling that's pressuring you and not act out of it?

212
00:22:55,485 --> 00:22:56,526
[SPEAKER_05]: You would give the right answer.

213
00:22:58,267 --> 00:23:02,189
[SPEAKER_05]: That's why it's basically gratuitous.

214
00:23:02,629 --> 00:23:05,650
[SPEAKER_04]: You might not be asking yourself that, though, necessarily.

215
00:23:05,790 --> 00:23:06,170
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

216
00:23:06,210 --> 00:23:08,651
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's the problem.

217
00:23:09,692 --> 00:23:17,475
[SPEAKER_05]: You know, but then you choose to pretty much put aside the clarity that you yourself have.

218
00:23:18,375 --> 00:23:22,677
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's a self-induced

219
00:23:25,775 --> 00:23:26,535
[SPEAKER_05]: Problem, let's say.

220
00:23:26,936 --> 00:23:33,059
[SPEAKER_05]: In the same way that the development of mindfulness cannot be... cannot happen accidentally.

221
00:23:33,979 --> 00:23:40,622
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that... that, you know, you just get put in the right environment and suddenly it develops on its own.

222
00:23:40,862 --> 00:23:46,105
[SPEAKER_05]: There is no way for that to... to build up unless you have been doing the right things.

223
00:23:46,665 --> 00:23:49,967
[SPEAKER_05]: You may not recognize it in hindsight that you were doing the right things because

224
00:23:51,286 --> 00:23:59,028
[SPEAKER_05]: Maybe you haven't had the right information sufficiently, or you haven't reflected on it enough, so you wouldn't be able to do it again.

225
00:23:59,628 --> 00:24:04,329
[SPEAKER_05]: But factually, it's something that you would have had to work for, let's say.

226
00:24:05,589 --> 00:24:14,111
[SPEAKER_05]: Because the direction of pressure, the direction of hindrances, is... like nobody will accidentally stop

227
00:24:15,468 --> 00:24:23,434
[SPEAKER_05]: Giving into that, because every reward that that anybody can conceivably know of, lies in that direction.

228
00:24:24,115 --> 00:24:25,836
[SPEAKER_05]: Lies in the direction of acting out.

229
00:24:28,358 --> 00:24:43,050
[SPEAKER_05]: So, without faith, without some degree of, let's say, higher motivation, there is no way that you can just endure that pain and maintain the clarity of feelings, the body, all the things this is talked about.

230
00:24:50,910 --> 00:24:53,031
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you know if you're acting out of hindrances?

231
00:24:53,812 --> 00:25:00,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, obviously, this is not something that would be externally defined by... in the way the precepts are.

232
00:25:00,656 --> 00:25:03,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, just don't do this, and you won't be acting out of hindrances.

233
00:25:03,817 --> 00:25:08,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, it's only to a certain extent, which is the extent of the eight precepts.

234
00:25:09,340 --> 00:25:11,061
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do those things, you're acting out of hindrances.

235
00:25:11,101 --> 00:25:17,385
[SPEAKER_05]: But beyond that, is there a way to know internally?

236
00:25:18,617 --> 00:25:21,518
[SPEAKER_01]: What is going to be rude and hindrance, and what is not?

237
00:25:35,181 --> 00:25:39,402
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, you could you could check whether that perspective is established.

238
00:25:40,842 --> 00:25:41,862
[SPEAKER_04]: That we've been talking about.

239
00:25:46,837 --> 00:25:49,059
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, but it is going to be included, because that's how mindfulness works.

240
00:25:49,079 --> 00:25:53,321
[SPEAKER_04]: When it's there, everything is included.

241
00:25:54,622 --> 00:25:54,742
[SPEAKER_04]: So...

242
00:26:16,929 --> 00:26:23,387
[SPEAKER_05]: How would you be able to know that this action that's being presented to you right now by your own mind,

243
00:26:24,373 --> 00:26:27,115
[SPEAKER_05]: That it's going to take you away from this clarity.

244
00:26:28,095 --> 00:26:29,696
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, that it's a hindrance.

245
00:26:31,077 --> 00:26:39,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't... because it's... you don't even need to use the... classify it into, oh, it's this type of hindrance, it's that type of hindrance.

246
00:26:40,423 --> 00:26:46,927
[SPEAKER_01]: You will be able to just directly see, this is the opposite direction of what I've cultivated.

247
00:26:47,767 --> 00:26:48,468
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how would you know?

248
00:26:54,840 --> 00:27:06,166
[SPEAKER_04]: What comes to mind if I was to abstain from following action, it would be unpleasant?

249
00:27:10,229 --> 00:27:11,769
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, that's one way you could look at it.

250
00:27:11,789 --> 00:27:17,873
[SPEAKER_05]: So, anything that the mind feels

251
00:27:19,287 --> 00:27:33,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Apprehensive, when you even tease the option of saying no to it, that already shows you that there's probably gratification and infatuation involved with that action.

252
00:27:34,850 --> 00:27:45,412
[SPEAKER_05]: So, because, see, that is what makes the mind unwilling to basically have the clarity that you yourself want to develop.

253
00:27:45,712 --> 00:27:47,612
[SPEAKER_05]: It's the fact that, like,

254
00:27:50,040 --> 00:28:00,750
[SPEAKER_05]: When you don't have that clarity, you can be having the same thoughts, and the same reflections, and the same encouragements for the mind, and it just ignores them.

255
00:28:01,010 --> 00:28:10,699
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't listen to them, and it insists in being concerned instead with sight, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, worries about the past, concerns about the future.

256
00:28:11,620 --> 00:28:15,723
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's when you basically

257
00:28:17,271 --> 00:28:19,573
[SPEAKER_05]: push it in any direction.

258
00:28:20,434 --> 00:28:29,123
[SPEAKER_05]: Actually, you would be able to know in advance, like, oh, this will... if I do this, the mind will become agitated.

259
00:28:29,803 --> 00:28:32,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, you could also say, the mind won't listen.

260
00:28:33,167 --> 00:28:37,110
[SPEAKER_00]: You can simply put, like, when you have a dog, and, you know, you kind of have it

261
00:28:37,931 --> 00:28:41,474
[SPEAKER_00]: on a closed leash, and it's listening to you, and it's kind of obedient.

262
00:28:42,115 --> 00:28:44,477
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you let it go a little bit.

263
00:28:45,398 --> 00:28:49,941
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, the more you let it go, the harder it's going to be.

264
00:28:50,782 --> 00:29:01,411
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, like, if you let it go, depending on the attitude that it has, depending on the behavior that it's showing, you can see, like, what that engagement will result in.

265
00:29:01,932 --> 00:29:04,374
[SPEAKER_00]: If the dog is calm, and you let it walk, well, that's fine.

266
00:29:05,317 --> 00:29:16,665
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you see that it's already getting in that kind of excitement mode, and you actually let it go, what you will see is, it's going to be three times harder to bring it back afterwards.

267
00:29:17,546 --> 00:29:19,287
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what you have to recognize.

268
00:29:19,307 --> 00:29:28,474
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to stop being concerned with, like, the kind of external definitions of what is allowable and what is not allowable.

269
00:29:28,934 --> 00:29:32,937
[SPEAKER_00]: If something... if you see that, at that moment,

270
00:29:34,072 --> 00:29:37,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Not yesterday, not tomorrow, not ten years ago.

271
00:29:38,041 --> 00:29:41,809
[SPEAKER_00]: At that moment, that dog is kind of...

272
00:29:42,665 --> 00:29:54,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting into that mode of agitation towards this action that might be externally praiseworthy and externally, you know, considered, whatever, virtuous, then you don't do it.

273
00:29:55,628 --> 00:30:11,072
[SPEAKER_00]: It is at that, like, what is a hindrance and what is not a hindrance, is going to be determined by your own mind, not by you and your kind of classification and your preference of... That's why it's...

274
00:30:12,523 --> 00:30:20,924
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, it is a fundamental truth or fundamental principle, but at the same time, that doesn't mean it's public.

275
00:30:21,855 --> 00:30:30,862
[SPEAKER_00]: It is to be experienced individually by the wise, because only in relation to your own mind can you know what is a hindrance and what is not, what is wholesome and what is unwholesome.

276
00:30:31,042 --> 00:30:31,303
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

277
00:30:31,483 --> 00:30:34,685
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's universal, but subjective.

278
00:30:34,926 --> 00:30:35,546
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, exactly.

279
00:30:35,786 --> 00:30:35,966
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

280
00:30:36,487 --> 00:30:46,315
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's... you cannot make a kind of... again, apart from the precepts, you cannot make a list of things that are always leading you astray.

281
00:30:46,635 --> 00:30:49,778
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that would be then the precepts, but within that...

282
00:30:50,658 --> 00:30:51,359
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

283
00:30:51,600 --> 00:31:02,713
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to determine from within the situation that you're in, which includes which feeling is present, what mood is going on there, which are precisely these satipaṭṭhāna, by the way.

284
00:31:03,414 --> 00:31:06,038
[SPEAKER_02]: Those, you want to keep an eye on, because

285
00:31:07,352 --> 00:31:08,512
[SPEAKER_02]: From day to day, it might change.

286
00:31:09,132 --> 00:31:09,392
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

287
00:31:09,953 --> 00:31:10,793
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what I was thinking.

288
00:31:10,853 --> 00:31:17,614
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if you could come up with the perfect list that is tailored specifically to your mind and your mind's tendencies, it'll just change.

289
00:31:17,654 --> 00:31:19,794
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't matter, because it changes from day to day.

290
00:31:20,034 --> 00:31:33,557
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's like you can make a list of things that, in the past, would have led to hindrances, and you may well overlook that your attitude of list building, at that very moment, is really aggressive.

291
00:31:34,485 --> 00:31:36,047
[SPEAKER_05]: Or doubt.

292
00:31:36,227 --> 00:31:40,134
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's something that the mind is, with that same agitation, engaging with.

293
00:31:41,035 --> 00:31:48,146
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it can only be seen presently and in that peripheral dimension of, like,

294
00:31:50,125 --> 00:31:53,247
[SPEAKER_05]: What is this going to do to my mind?

295
00:31:53,287 --> 00:31:56,189
[SPEAKER_05]: Is it going to make it more obedient or less obedient?

296
00:31:56,289 --> 00:31:57,310
[SPEAKER_05]: Just on that general level.

297
00:31:57,730 --> 00:32:05,455
[SPEAKER_05]: You don't even have to think in terms of, like, how does this action relate to the body or feelings or something like that.

298
00:32:06,956 --> 00:32:15,484
[SPEAKER_05]: Is this animal going to listen to me less and be concerned with the world as a result of me letting it do this?

299
00:32:15,624 --> 00:32:29,517
[SPEAKER_05]: And even if those things that it's concerned with are not as bad as the things that you let go through the precepts, let's say, it's still... the fact that it is going to become disobedient, that is bad in and of itself.

300
00:32:30,183 --> 00:32:37,370
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter if it becomes disobedient for, like, you know, things that are... that are... that other people may regard as fine.

301
00:32:38,091 --> 00:32:44,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the suttas would say that, eh... like a noble disciple would be... would be, eh...

302
00:32:45,458 --> 00:32:56,632
[SPEAKER_05]: It's basically hindered from progressing further if they become overly engaged with talk, sleep and work, which none of them are against the precepts.

303
00:32:57,874 --> 00:33:01,698
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's the fact that they kind of...

304
00:33:03,643 --> 00:33:20,500
[SPEAKER_05]: Again, push the mind into that mode where it's not going to listen to you and where it's not going to be pliable for developing the contexts and taking it into directions that it has to go, which is dispassion, relinquishment, cessation.

305
00:33:21,060 --> 00:33:22,542
[SPEAKER_05]: That alone is a problem.

306
00:33:23,525 --> 00:33:25,445
[SPEAKER_05]: That it's going to make the mind disobedient.

307
00:33:25,505 --> 00:33:39,229
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that needs to be... Once the precepts are established, the standard needs to be upgraded to that much more general level of, like, leading to pliability of mind, leading away from pliability of mind.

308
00:33:39,529 --> 00:33:48,991
[SPEAKER_05]: And... and, eh... it's... it's... it's important as well to see that the...

309
00:33:50,818 --> 00:33:54,660
[SPEAKER_05]: Attitude of denying everything, that's another trap, actually.

310
00:33:55,500 --> 00:34:00,963
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, it's not that if you say no to everything, you're going to make it that much more pliable.

311
00:34:01,664 --> 00:34:06,546
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, if I could just say no to everything, then, oh, well, everything's fixed, right?

312
00:34:06,586 --> 00:34:09,227
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, if only it were that easy.

313
00:34:09,528 --> 00:34:13,690
[SPEAKER_05]: It's because, in and of itself, that

314
00:34:14,590 --> 00:34:20,437
[SPEAKER_01]: engagement of saying no to things, that very much can be written in aversion, which is another one of the hindrances.

315
00:34:21,258 --> 00:34:29,887
[SPEAKER_05]: So, um... it's it's a it's a kind of balancing act, almost, and you have to

316
00:34:31,143 --> 00:34:39,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Not even trial and error, because trial and error kind of implies that you... that you will find like a fixed answer at a certain point.

317
00:34:39,667 --> 00:34:45,889
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not... you don't find an answer to... to what is a hindrance and what isn't, and how to calm the mind.

318
00:34:46,330 --> 00:34:54,113
[SPEAKER_01]: You just understand the broader principle that you're able to apply in each individual circumstance.

319
00:35:04,766 --> 00:35:16,689
[SPEAKER_04]: That training of the mind, or making it more pliable, as we were saying, is that only discerned through... like, does that have to be discerned through feeling?

320
00:35:17,409 --> 00:35:20,910
[SPEAKER_04]: So it's not like I can just think about it abstractly.

321
00:35:21,350 --> 00:35:27,692
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that has to be... yeah, to know what choice to make has to be felt.

322
00:35:29,643 --> 00:35:32,765
[SPEAKER_05]: Basically, yeah, you have to feel it, quote-unquote, at that moment.

323
00:35:32,785 --> 00:35:35,127
[SPEAKER_05]: You have to feel... because that's the thing.

324
00:35:35,167 --> 00:35:41,633
[SPEAKER_05]: The mind is... like... it's not something that you would be able to overlook.

325
00:35:42,433 --> 00:35:45,756
[SPEAKER_05]: Or, let's say, it's... you would certainly be able to overlook it.

326
00:35:46,156 --> 00:35:49,459
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not something that can be actually absent from your experience.

327
00:35:49,879 --> 00:35:50,400
[SPEAKER_01]: It is there.

328
00:35:50,900 --> 00:35:56,565
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just that if you don't look in that direction, like... it's just a simple kind of...

329
00:35:57,803 --> 00:36:13,030
[SPEAKER_05]: It's a glimpse of how is the mind kind of reacting to this, or what kind of disposition or behavior is it showing when it looks in that direction.

330
00:36:14,370 --> 00:36:24,555
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's that feeling that's felt at that moment, if it's, as the suttas would say, the joy and pleasure on account of

331
00:36:25,902 --> 00:36:29,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Mundane things, that's what you shouldn't cultivate.

332
00:36:30,104 --> 00:36:34,846
[SPEAKER_01]: He said that pleasure should be feared, should be shunned, should be avoided.

333
00:36:35,726 --> 00:36:44,569
[SPEAKER_05]: Not the external sense objects, but the feeling that you see on the level of the mind, when it's inclining towards those things.

334
00:36:44,629 --> 00:36:46,390
[SPEAKER_05]: That's what has to be avoided.

335
00:36:48,611 --> 00:36:53,813
[SPEAKER_05]: And on the other hand, if there's no feeling there, that there's no feeling of pleasure on that mental level,

336
00:36:55,336 --> 00:37:02,899
[SPEAKER_01]: When the mind is, let's say, I don't know, the classic example, it wants to go for a walk.

337
00:37:04,339 --> 00:37:08,901
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's no like kind of obsessive sense of pleasure associated with it.

338
00:37:09,661 --> 00:37:12,122
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't really care that much if you don't do it either.

339
00:37:13,436 --> 00:37:17,460
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, you have to see, oh wait, well, I don't have to say no to this.

340
00:37:19,202 --> 00:37:27,710
[SPEAKER_05]: Because if you do, then you're... if you do say no to it, it means you're emphasizing something other than the actual discernment of the mind.

341
00:37:27,850 --> 00:37:29,973
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just using your preconceived notions of

342
00:37:30,673 --> 00:37:33,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I shouldn't do this, because such and such.

343
00:37:35,415 --> 00:37:50,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and on the other hand as well, with aversion, like if you, if you realize, let's say, avoid, avoiding certain types of discomfort.

344
00:37:52,042 --> 00:37:53,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when is that actually aversion?

345
00:37:54,823 --> 00:37:56,124
[SPEAKER_01]: And when is it just

346
00:37:58,765 --> 00:38:01,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting according to intentions.

347
00:38:01,488 --> 00:38:02,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's that's how you would know.

348
00:38:03,610 --> 00:38:09,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, is the mind internally upset and agitated about this?

349
00:38:09,256 --> 00:38:13,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, is it going to kind of rebel and complain?

350
00:38:14,802 --> 00:38:19,146
[SPEAKER_01]: If I don't let it avoid this, well, then you may want to think twice about

351
00:38:22,413 --> 00:38:24,393
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of like thinking you can train a dog.

352
00:38:24,413 --> 00:38:27,154
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, when it does something wrong, you hate it.

353
00:38:52,908 --> 00:38:55,089
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, it's not doing anything wrong.

354
00:38:55,309 --> 00:38:58,151
[SPEAKER_03]: And you think, well, might as well hit it.

355
00:38:58,271 --> 00:38:59,431
[SPEAKER_03]: It will make it more obedient.

356
00:39:00,732 --> 00:39:00,952
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

357
00:39:01,112 --> 00:39:03,093
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't put your hands just confusing the door.

358
00:39:04,033 --> 00:39:10,497
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, it will fear you, but it won't go, well, not for the right reasons.

359
00:39:10,857 --> 00:39:11,757
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but now...

360
00:39:11,837 --> 00:39:12,558
[SPEAKER_03]: It won't be obedient.

361
00:39:12,658 --> 00:39:14,599
[SPEAKER_03]: It just won't do...

362
00:39:14,619 --> 00:39:17,540
[SPEAKER_03]: It won't go anywhere, so to say, even where you would want it to go.

363
00:39:18,507 --> 00:39:25,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but if you only hit it when it is actually doing something wrong, then the message, what is wrong, would be clearer to the dog.

364
00:39:25,735 --> 00:39:28,618
[SPEAKER_02]: Then it has been clouded by just a random slap here and there.

365
00:39:28,638 --> 00:39:31,140
[SPEAKER_02]: The dog would just be confused, like, what's wrong with this now?

366
00:39:31,220 --> 00:39:35,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so the thing is, that's why it's then important to to put aside

367
00:39:36,926 --> 00:39:42,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Your notions of what's right and wrong, and base everything upon the mind.

368
00:39:42,594 --> 00:39:46,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, discernment of the present mental state, the present mood.

369
00:39:46,839 --> 00:39:47,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it effective with greed?

370
00:39:48,201 --> 00:39:49,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it effective with aversion?

371
00:39:49,803 --> 00:39:52,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it effective with delusion?

372
00:39:52,166 --> 00:39:52,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Because...

373
00:39:53,988 --> 00:39:58,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, as we've discussed before, that's how you would also overcome sīla-bhāta-paramāṁsa.

374
00:39:58,131 --> 00:40:16,003
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you would cease to emphasize any sort of preconceived notions of what... of sense of duty or sense of, like, being... being... needing to... to adhere to some kind of external standard of behavior.

375
00:40:16,083 --> 00:40:21,287
[SPEAKER_00]: And instead, everything would be automatically oriented by that vision of

376
00:40:22,680 --> 00:40:28,942
[SPEAKER_01]: How is the mind inclining in relation to this action at this moment?

377
00:40:29,102 --> 00:40:30,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in an abstract sense.

378
00:40:32,623 --> 00:40:43,007
[SPEAKER_04]: I was kind of thinking about, throughout this discussion, when Ajahn Yanamoli would say, like, it doesn't matter, kind of, if you can handle it.

379
00:40:43,127 --> 00:40:45,768
[SPEAKER_04]: It's if the mind can handle it.

380
00:40:45,968 --> 00:40:46,388
[SPEAKER_04]: That is...

381
00:40:47,573 --> 00:40:50,114
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it kind of ties into like, oh, yeah, it's fine if I do this.

382
00:40:50,234 --> 00:40:53,415
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, that's not really what you should be focusing on.

383
00:40:54,695 --> 00:40:55,336
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

384
00:40:55,356 --> 00:40:57,876
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, what is the consequence going to be for the mind?

385
00:40:57,956 --> 00:40:58,197
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

386
00:40:58,217 --> 00:41:01,177
[SPEAKER_04]: When you do this, which is not necessarily going to be like it.

387
00:41:10,020 --> 00:41:11,381
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not going to be as...

388
00:41:13,865 --> 00:41:15,307
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, within your control.

389
00:41:15,688 --> 00:41:28,908
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, like we said just now, the greater issue comes from the fact that the mind, if it's factually developed to some degree of perspective, you may not even feel the pain right away.

390
00:41:29,789 --> 00:41:31,690
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, factually, the mind becomes immovable.

391
00:41:32,290 --> 00:41:36,953
[SPEAKER_05]: So, then, you will realize only afterwards.

392
00:41:37,593 --> 00:41:43,516
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, when the... oh, wait, I'm sitting in the spit of burning coals again.

393
00:41:43,896 --> 00:41:46,017
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you... you...

394
00:41:47,258 --> 00:41:48,298
[SPEAKER_05]: But, that's the thing.

395
00:41:48,318 --> 00:41:55,162
[SPEAKER_05]: You can see already, even before you go through with it, you factually can see, that is where the mind will end up.

396
00:41:56,798 --> 00:42:02,568
[SPEAKER_05]: It may not be immediate, because if it were immediate, well, you probably wouldn't go through with it.

397
00:42:02,668 --> 00:42:08,237
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's precisely because it's kind of like far away, and you think like, oh, no, no, I'll kind of...

398
00:42:08,257 --> 00:42:09,199
[SPEAKER_05]: I'll get back on track.

399
00:42:10,223 --> 00:42:12,846
[SPEAKER_05]: Way before I end up in that direction.

400
00:42:13,607 --> 00:42:14,608
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's why you let it go.

401
00:42:15,448 --> 00:42:18,151
[SPEAKER_05]: And you don't realize, like, it's like a snowball.

402
00:42:18,591 --> 00:42:23,777
[SPEAKER_05]: So, just one little bit is enough to just keep rolling.

403
00:42:24,297 --> 00:42:26,619
[SPEAKER_05]: You could stop yourself, factually, but why?

404
00:42:27,727 --> 00:42:40,058
[SPEAKER_05]: Who, in their right mind, would start exciting a dog that's twice heavier than them, to want to go in the opposite direction where they want them to be in?

405
00:42:40,839 --> 00:42:48,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if they can't stop it, why would they risk being dragged to where they don't want to go?

406
00:42:56,394 --> 00:43:09,881
[SPEAKER_03]: And what you said about the sīlabate paramāsa and seeing, well, seeing, giving up your idea of this external thing is how I train my mind.

407
00:43:13,643 --> 00:43:23,948
[SPEAKER_03]: I would say it's more, well, more so than giving up what you think is this external thing that you are doing,

408
00:43:25,142 --> 00:43:28,563
[SPEAKER_03]: It's more about questioning what you think external means.

409
00:43:30,243 --> 00:43:32,884
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, like, oh, obviously I'm not doing those things.

410
00:43:33,064 --> 00:43:37,605
[SPEAKER_03]: Failing to see, well, there must be something you are doing in that way.

411
00:43:37,745 --> 00:43:48,468
[SPEAKER_05]: Otherwise, you would have seen... Yeah, because external, of course, you know, that's what we always end up coming back to, like this common notion of rites and rituals.

412
00:43:48,948 --> 00:43:51,408
[SPEAKER_05]: That's like, oh, the obviously external thing.

413
00:43:52,028 --> 00:43:54,429
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, to some extent, you realize that

414
00:43:55,288 --> 00:44:02,115
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, if you're half-wise, you realize, yeah, that's not where the training lies.

415
00:44:02,856 --> 00:44:05,238
[SPEAKER_05]: But, like, what is actually external?

416
00:44:05,799 --> 00:44:08,061
[SPEAKER_05]: External could very well be still on the level of

417
00:44:09,880 --> 00:44:35,741
[SPEAKER_05]: Internal, quote-unquote, practices of, like, the ways that you think you need to be reflecting or contemplating, or whatever, or the ways that you think you should be watching your intentions, or directing the mind like this, or directing the mind like that, all of that would be made external if you don't see the actual inclination of the mind at that moment.

418
00:44:36,382 --> 00:44:39,144
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, like you might have a sense of duty, for example,

419
00:44:39,847 --> 00:44:52,594
[SPEAKER_02]: Built up in the course of your life, and now you are in a situation when nothing properly external is insisting that you should perform the duty, but you still do it because you regard it as duty.

420
00:44:52,754 --> 00:45:00,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but even on a more accurate level, even if externally you're not... it's nothing that anybody else can see.

421
00:45:01,292 --> 00:45:02,072
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fully mental.

422
00:45:02,652 --> 00:45:03,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

423
00:45:03,953 --> 00:45:07,293
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would... it would still be an external sīla-bhātta.

424
00:45:07,433 --> 00:45:11,314
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be an external sense of duty, of... could be anything.

425
00:45:11,354 --> 00:45:19,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Could be, I don't know, reflecting on death, or every day I have to be, you know, reflecting on the drawbacks and shattering.

426
00:45:19,876 --> 00:45:21,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

427
00:45:21,436 --> 00:45:26,817
[SPEAKER_05]: And those things are... you see, you're not even seeing, like, how is the mind planning?

428
00:45:26,977 --> 00:45:28,017
[SPEAKER_05]: Is it actually working?

429
00:45:28,277 --> 00:45:29,278
[SPEAKER_05]: Where is it taking me?

430
00:45:29,938 --> 00:45:30,398
[SPEAKER_05]: You just...

431
00:45:31,768 --> 00:45:38,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Take on this kind of repetitive performance of a certain thing.

432
00:45:38,953 --> 00:45:47,858
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you get sick for half a week, or for a week, and you can't do your particular exercises, and then you feel that you are not fulfilling your duty as a monk.

433
00:45:48,479 --> 00:45:50,120
[SPEAKER_02]: And then that becomes unpleasant.

434
00:45:50,922 --> 00:45:54,344
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, the whole thing just goes downhill, because from the start it was very wrong.

435
00:45:54,424 --> 00:45:54,584
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

436
00:45:54,684 --> 00:46:08,692
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to see on the acute, internal, presently arisen level, eh... as... you have to use that as a standard for what needs to be done and what doesn't need to be done.

437
00:46:09,212 --> 00:46:11,993
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you would realize it works both ways.

438
00:46:12,294 --> 00:46:17,516
[SPEAKER_05]: It works both in the sense that sometimes you might be overdoing it.

439
00:46:17,857 --> 00:46:20,398
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, sometimes you might be... the mind is already

440
00:46:21,022 --> 00:46:30,648
[SPEAKER_00]: In the direction of, you know, clarity and context, but you keep pushing it and prodding it with these reflections that already serve their purpose.

441
00:46:31,549 --> 00:46:32,029
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

442
00:46:32,089 --> 00:46:36,712
[SPEAKER_02]: Which would be like, well, that would then become aimless or restlessness, for example.

443
00:46:36,732 --> 00:46:36,872
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

444
00:46:36,912 --> 00:46:38,893
[SPEAKER_02]: You just don't know when it's okay.

445
00:46:39,253 --> 00:46:46,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, it could work the other way around as well, that, you know, you you think, like, often with the kind of, um,

446
00:46:47,398 --> 00:46:52,261
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea that meditation is like this session that you do during the day.

447
00:46:52,741 --> 00:46:54,502
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it will work both ways.

448
00:46:54,542 --> 00:46:58,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So, during the session, you think that you have to be focusing every moment.

449
00:46:58,804 --> 00:47:06,228
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't realize, like, where, when the mind is factually already clear about the context.

450
00:47:06,249 --> 00:47:08,510
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to keep repeating it anymore.

451
00:47:09,528 --> 00:47:14,429
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, you would think that when the session ends, that you can just do whatever you want.

452
00:47:14,850 --> 00:47:15,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

453
00:47:15,250 --> 00:47:20,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that actually, no, you can't press pause on the mind.

454
00:47:21,051 --> 00:47:21,251
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

455
00:47:21,371 --> 00:47:27,373
[SPEAKER_02]: But I've done my duty, I've done my hour in the morning, and I've done my puja in the evening, so what happens during the rest of the day?

456
00:47:27,413 --> 00:47:27,893
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter.

457
00:47:27,973 --> 00:47:28,754
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

458
00:47:28,814 --> 00:47:31,774
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you do two hours in the morning, then for the rest of the day you're fine.

459
00:47:31,814 --> 00:47:32,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

460
00:47:32,935 --> 00:47:33,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

461
00:47:34,192 --> 00:47:37,155
[SPEAKER_00]: At any given time, you will have to be clear about this.

462
00:47:37,315 --> 00:47:41,378
[SPEAKER_00]: And this might... this very reflection might become mechanical at some point.

463
00:47:41,558 --> 00:47:46,903
[SPEAKER_05]: The reflection of where the intention is and how the mind is inclined towards things.

464
00:47:46,943 --> 00:47:50,065
[SPEAKER_05]: That will become your external practice at some point.

465
00:47:50,606 --> 00:47:52,988
[SPEAKER_05]: But you always have a chance.

466
00:47:53,869 --> 00:48:00,274
[SPEAKER_05]: If you have been keeping the virtue, if you have been keeping the senses straight, and you know that you have to be self-honest,

467
00:48:00,923 --> 00:48:07,644
[SPEAKER_05]: At some point, it will land on you that, oh, wait, I'm doing this now with this insane external sense of duty.

468
00:48:07,664 --> 00:48:07,924
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

469
00:48:09,645 --> 00:48:12,425
[SPEAKER_05]: And then you would see, oh, okay, so that's where the mind is inclining.

470
00:48:12,605 --> 00:48:17,346
[SPEAKER_05]: It wasn't like everything else that I was assuming to be the inclination of the mind was secondary.

471
00:48:17,446 --> 00:48:22,347
[SPEAKER_05]: It was this very pressure that was directing me to do this.

472
00:48:22,427 --> 00:48:23,107
[SPEAKER_05]: That's where it was.

473
00:48:23,987 --> 00:48:24,847
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's kind of the thing.

474
00:48:24,967 --> 00:48:26,687
[SPEAKER_03]: It's kind of the ironic bit.

475
00:48:28,708 --> 00:48:29,168
[SPEAKER_03]: All of those

476
00:48:30,313 --> 00:48:37,955
[SPEAKER_03]: Things that you would be doing wrongly, to address the issue, you're doing precisely so as not to address the issue.

477
00:48:38,035 --> 00:48:38,395
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

478
00:48:39,035 --> 00:48:41,916
[SPEAKER_03]: So as to ignore the state of the mind.

479
00:48:43,096 --> 00:48:47,237
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, see, not acting out of restlessness is painful.

480
00:48:47,417 --> 00:48:49,157
[SPEAKER_05]: And so that's what you're doing right then and there.

481
00:48:49,997 --> 00:48:54,998
[SPEAKER_05]: And, sure, you might... if somebody asks you, you would... oh, what am I doing?

482
00:48:55,018 --> 00:48:57,199
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah, I'm trying to not act out of the hindrances.

483
00:48:57,877 --> 00:49:04,419
[SPEAKER_05]: But, yeah, what the mind says it's doing, and what it's actually doing, is two different things.

484
00:49:05,219 --> 00:49:14,622
[SPEAKER_05]: And you need to be able to discern the latter, and not go by just what the mind kind of... what you initially... when you ask yourself, what am I doing?

485
00:49:14,642 --> 00:49:19,124
[SPEAKER_05]: The first answer that comes, that's not actually the intention.

486
00:49:19,544 --> 00:49:23,245
[SPEAKER_01]: When we talk about reflecting on intentions, I think we clarified that before, that

487
00:49:25,250 --> 00:49:33,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Usually, you might think it's like the reasons, the reasoning behind, like the explanation behind this action.

488
00:49:34,473 --> 00:49:34,833
[SPEAKER_05]: But no.

489
00:49:35,153 --> 00:49:39,914
[SPEAKER_05]: You'll be more accurate to say, like, what would happen if I just stop this?

490
00:49:42,495 --> 00:49:51,878
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what, that's where, that's really, that kind of reflection would indirectly reveal what present inclination there is.

491
00:49:53,065 --> 00:49:55,607
[SPEAKER_01]: What happens if I don't do it?

492
00:49:57,709 --> 00:50:01,691
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what would determine the extent of mindfulness as well.

493
00:50:02,172 --> 00:50:06,175
[SPEAKER_01]: When you realize, oh, whether I do it or don't do it, it doesn't matter.

494
00:50:06,255 --> 00:50:07,175
[SPEAKER_05]: The clarity is there.

495
00:50:07,816 --> 00:50:09,037
[SPEAKER_05]: And then you need to protect it.

496
00:50:09,077 --> 00:50:20,465
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, if it's, you know, in the direction of sensuality, in the direction of aversion, even if, at that moment, it won't be intense aversion that you

497
00:50:21,006 --> 00:50:32,455
[SPEAKER_05]: Or intense sensuality that you gave up initially, you have to realize, well, but even a little bit is going to start taking me back to where I worked so hard to get away from, so why would I go back there?

498
00:50:33,877 --> 00:50:40,142
[SPEAKER_05]: And that now becomes the way, another way for you to strengthen the mindfulness even more.

499
00:50:40,282 --> 00:50:46,487
[SPEAKER_05]: So, just simply by abstaining from those actions, the perspective will grow even stronger, basically.

500
00:50:52,013 --> 00:51:05,366
[SPEAKER_03]: But also, if you see that, the mind's inclination, you could just as well ask, like, how will accepting this affect the mind?

501
00:51:07,829 --> 00:51:14,095
[SPEAKER_03]: As opposed to... Like, you could ask yourself, oh, will I get upset?

502
00:51:14,375 --> 00:51:15,456
[SPEAKER_03]: Or will the mind get upset?

503
00:51:16,410 --> 00:51:17,951
[SPEAKER_03]: If I refuse to accept this...

504
00:51:18,592 --> 00:51:27,200
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, if you see it, even if you ask yourself, just like, oh, but even... how would the mind... where would the mind go if I accept this?

505
00:51:27,320 --> 00:51:28,721
[SPEAKER_03]: You would see the same.

506
00:51:28,761 --> 00:51:34,266
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just kind of a different aspect of the same intention.

507
00:51:36,889 --> 00:51:41,112
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the mind would present some option.

508
00:51:44,763 --> 00:51:57,987
[SPEAKER_03]: And entertaining, let's say, like mentally, like thinking, how where accepting would lead, would reveal it just as much as thinking about where refusing that would lead.

509
00:51:58,607 --> 00:51:59,427
[SPEAKER_03]: Aha, I see what you mean.

510
00:51:59,467 --> 00:52:00,468
[SPEAKER_03]: Just the different...

511
00:52:00,868 --> 00:52:08,650
[SPEAKER_03]: But the thing is, without some perspective, when you would entertain accepting it, you would just be overwhelmed by the...

512
00:52:09,438 --> 00:52:12,901
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the pleasure that's often then there is, like, basically falling into.

513
00:52:12,961 --> 00:52:14,122
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, you would see...

514
00:52:14,682 --> 00:52:15,503
[SPEAKER_05]: The thing is, eh...

515
00:52:17,985 --> 00:52:22,188
[SPEAKER_05]: If you question yourself... Like, you might have already accepted it mentally.

516
00:52:22,868 --> 00:52:27,051
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's why, more often than not, contemplating what happens if I just don't do it.

517
00:52:27,151 --> 00:52:29,173
[SPEAKER_05]: What happens if...

518
00:52:29,193 --> 00:52:29,593
[SPEAKER_05]: This thing...

519
00:52:29,653 --> 00:52:31,475
[SPEAKER_05]: I never get to experience this thing ever again.

520
00:52:32,783 --> 00:52:45,153
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, that will reveal to you... because the direction of welcoming and accepting, it's kind of already bound with... and it's also already bound with a diminishing perspective.

521
00:52:45,253 --> 00:52:51,838
[SPEAKER_05]: So then, to the extent that you already went in that direction, you won't be able to be clear about the fact that you are going in that direction.

522
00:52:52,418 --> 00:52:57,162
[SPEAKER_05]: I get... it kind of muddles your own self-awareness, in other words.

523
00:52:58,203 --> 00:53:00,885
[SPEAKER_05]: That's why you can't engage in sensuality mindfully.

524
00:53:01,626 --> 00:53:04,228
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, having perspective over your sensuality.

525
00:53:05,149 --> 00:53:07,330
[SPEAKER_01]: That's two incompatible things.

526
00:53:07,591 --> 00:53:09,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality means not having perspective.

527
00:53:10,193 --> 00:53:22,342
[SPEAKER_05]: So, more often than not, if you ask yourself, not even just bodily, physically, kind of giving in, what happens if I just don't think about this?

528
00:53:23,243 --> 00:53:24,464
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would already say, like, oh, wait.

529
00:53:25,685 --> 00:53:27,006
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there was a compulsion there.

530
00:53:28,024 --> 00:53:32,721
[SPEAKER_05]: And, eh... if you were going by your... again, your...

531
00:53:33,439 --> 00:53:38,964
[SPEAKER_05]: If you had preconceived ideas of what's right and wrong, you might have thought, well, this is not a precept.

532
00:53:39,084 --> 00:53:44,808
[SPEAKER_05]: This is even like, kind of, praiseworthy action.

533
00:53:44,988 --> 00:53:46,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I'm helping other people or something.

534
00:53:47,290 --> 00:53:55,557
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would have emphasized that, rather than clearly, like, the mind is itching.

535
00:53:55,637 --> 00:53:58,760
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is the way that it's scratching itself right now.

536
00:54:10,654 --> 00:54:27,202
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, like when you have inclinations of like overly denying things, like with kind of self-mortification, and you ask yourself like, oh, so what would happen if I stopped doing these?

537
00:54:28,033 --> 00:54:30,114
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's another compulsion.

538
00:54:54,842 --> 00:54:58,666
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that cannot possibly be the direction where actual peace lies.

539
00:55:00,969 --> 00:55:14,643
[SPEAKER_05]: It might be less blinding than sensuality, just because relaxing is always easier than tightening, simply because of that.

540
00:55:15,324 --> 00:55:16,105
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh...

541
00:55:16,636 --> 00:55:27,407
[SPEAKER_01]: To the extent that the mind is... to the extent that there's concern, to the extent that there's need, to the extent that there's a dependence, it's a problem.

542
00:55:27,707 --> 00:55:28,869
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not what nibbāna is.

543
00:55:28,949 --> 00:55:39,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize, oh, if I cannot survive without denying things that are not even

544
00:55:40,942 --> 00:55:53,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Greed, aversion and delusion in themselves, that's that's a sign of the same hindrances.

545
00:55:53,170 --> 00:56:02,857
[SPEAKER_04]: This also applies to when the mind starts to leap up at renunciation, as it says.

546
00:56:02,937 --> 00:56:04,558
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't at first, but eventually.

547
00:56:05,348 --> 00:56:08,450
[SPEAKER_04]: Because one progresses and sees that as pleasant.

548
00:56:09,171 --> 00:56:14,695
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, it's about how... how close... oh, yeah.

549
00:56:15,636 --> 00:56:16,877
[SPEAKER_04]: Refining that renunciation.

550
00:56:17,097 --> 00:56:18,398
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not just this kind of...

551
00:56:18,998 --> 00:56:21,340
[SPEAKER_04]: I was going to give things up for the sake of them.

552
00:56:21,881 --> 00:56:28,205
[SPEAKER_04]: For the sake of... like, okay, well, I'm not going to... just... eat with a spoon anymore.

553
00:56:28,225 --> 00:56:28,786
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

554
00:56:28,826 --> 00:56:29,106
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

555
00:56:29,386 --> 00:56:32,268
[SPEAKER_04]: Just, eh... just give in to that.

556
00:56:34,050 --> 00:56:34,210
[SPEAKER_04]: And...

557
00:56:36,945 --> 00:56:50,929
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, if it's the actual renunciation on that level, you would see that it's... if you renounce the external things, it would be coming from the other direction.

558
00:56:50,949 --> 00:56:55,770
[SPEAKER_05]: It would be coming from the direction of seeing how that will affect the mind.

559
00:56:56,892 --> 00:57:04,494
[SPEAKER_05]: You won't be giving things up just because you heard that there was this great monk who also gave these things up.

560
00:57:04,514 --> 00:57:11,595
[SPEAKER_05]: You would see directly, for this mind, giving this up is going to lead to more awesome states.

561
00:57:13,096 --> 00:57:14,676
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not usually what people do.

562
00:57:14,956 --> 00:57:24,878
[SPEAKER_01]: They do it on... they have the inspiration to give things up, which is good, but then they judge what has to be given up by all sorts of external

563
00:57:26,202 --> 00:57:35,407
[SPEAKER_01]: It sounds silly, but generally, it often is like, oh, I heard that there was such and such, and that's what he did, so let me do it too.

564
00:57:36,367 --> 00:57:41,030
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's no direct discernment of like, okay, but is this actually

565
00:57:42,718 --> 00:57:53,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Increasing my perspective is actually... Do I see that on account of doing this, the mind doesn't incline to sensual thoughts anymore?

566
00:57:54,581 --> 00:58:02,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Or am I just like occupying myself with this other stuff and that's why the sensual thoughts arise... don't arise anymore?

567
00:58:04,863 --> 00:58:10,569
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just occupied with, I don't know, traveling around and living in the forest.

568
00:58:11,350 --> 00:58:19,338
[SPEAKER_01]: And because I always have something to do and the next thing to take care of and worry about, that's why I don't have essential thoughts coming up.

569
00:58:19,358 --> 00:58:26,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because, you know, you actually set up the renunciation properly and then the mind is generally turned away from that whole domain.

570
00:58:33,665 --> 00:58:38,206
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, so in connection with this, can you be hindered by just one hindrance?

571
00:58:41,027 --> 00:58:45,448
[SPEAKER_02]: Because sometimes people ask this question, like, what's your dominant hindrance?

572
00:58:45,808 --> 00:58:51,069
[SPEAKER_02]: Having the impression, like, okay, so all the other four hindrances, I'm sort of, you know, there's just this one.

573
00:58:52,069 --> 00:58:54,310
[SPEAKER_02]: Would that be an accurate discernment?

574
00:59:04,347 --> 00:59:06,088
[SPEAKER_04]: Maybe center stage.

575
00:59:09,251 --> 00:59:10,772
[SPEAKER_04]: That is a kind of throne.

576
00:59:10,792 --> 00:59:10,892
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

577
00:59:11,953 --> 00:59:13,214
[SPEAKER_04]: That particular entrance.

578
00:59:13,374 --> 00:59:13,614
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

579
00:59:13,994 --> 00:59:14,915
[SPEAKER_02]: That you can identify.

580
00:59:15,655 --> 00:59:25,703
[SPEAKER_02]: But can that be, like... Is it possible for someone that... Can it be like, okay, there's just this entrance, center stage, and the other four, they're just not there now?

581
00:59:28,199 --> 00:59:32,022
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, you would be relating to that hindrance in some way.

582
00:59:32,122 --> 00:59:35,184
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it could be with the same hindrance or it could be with a different one.

583
00:59:36,746 --> 00:59:42,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the thing is, of course, they are five.

584
00:59:42,710 --> 00:59:42,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

585
00:59:44,031 --> 00:59:52,278
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a way, you can recognize specific manifestations of hindrances.

586
00:59:53,791 --> 00:59:59,235
[SPEAKER_00]: But it works the same way as what we said before with the clarity of mindfulness.

587
00:59:59,936 --> 01:00:07,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's not... you could say you have liability to clarity or liability to muddleness.

588
01:00:09,143 --> 01:00:12,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, inclination towards clarity or inclination towards muddleness.

589
01:00:12,626 --> 01:00:17,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And inclination towards clarity is the opposite of hindrances.

590
01:00:18,430 --> 01:00:21,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, it doesn't matter what specifically that clarity is.

591
01:00:24,287 --> 01:00:31,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Directed towards, the point is, there is the broader sense of clarity there, and the same with the hindrances.

592
01:00:31,910 --> 01:00:38,552
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter which one of them, specifically, or at least a determining factor is not going to be which one of them is there.

593
01:00:39,373 --> 01:00:43,974
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the fact that you're liable to have... Well, that you're effectively hindrance.

594
01:00:44,094 --> 01:00:44,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

595
01:00:45,035 --> 01:00:50,357
[SPEAKER_05]: So then, whichever, like, for someone who gives in to sensuality,

596
01:00:53,248 --> 01:00:56,431
[SPEAKER_00]: If bad things happen to them, they will be that much more prone to get angry.

597
01:00:57,552 --> 01:01:14,507
[SPEAKER_00]: If, you know, there is some sort of threat to something that they value, there will be that much... of course, because that's what sensuality is, valuing and being attached to things, there will be that much more of a liability to be worried and concerned about it.

598
01:01:15,688 --> 01:01:19,512
[SPEAKER_00]: And they don't have the stimulation that they're used to.

599
01:01:20,873 --> 01:01:23,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the whole laziness ends.

600
01:01:23,134 --> 01:01:25,055
[SPEAKER_00]: It's nothing until it comes up.

601
01:01:26,055 --> 01:01:38,260
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would see the same works the other way around, like when somebody has been giving in to aversion, or even sadness, depression, and all these things.

602
01:01:38,340 --> 01:01:39,580
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it that they look for?

603
01:01:41,021 --> 01:01:44,402
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it that they look for to uplift their mood and sensuality?

604
01:01:45,655 --> 01:01:48,597
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is where the mood went down.

605
01:01:48,617 --> 01:01:50,879
[SPEAKER_02]: To begin with, yeah.

606
01:01:52,340 --> 01:02:02,709
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you've been giving in to doubts and concerns and worries, the same thing happens.

607
01:02:03,029 --> 01:02:13,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the first promise of peace that you're going to see is, oh, let me just find some pleasant sense object to engage with, to calm the mind down.

608
01:02:15,118 --> 01:02:21,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... that's... that's not the type of confidence that you want.

609
01:02:21,022 --> 01:02:21,202
[SPEAKER_02]: Uh-huh.

610
01:02:21,222 --> 01:02:22,562
[SPEAKER_02]: You keep yourself within the indices.

611
01:02:22,622 --> 01:02:23,283
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

612
01:02:23,363 --> 01:02:25,284
[SPEAKER_05]: You... you... well, that's the thing.

613
01:02:25,324 --> 01:02:32,548
[SPEAKER_00]: You kind of, eh... pushing away the specific manifestation by doing this action.

614
01:02:32,608 --> 01:02:37,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, so momentarily, it might, eh... cover it up.

615
01:02:37,411 --> 01:02:38,872
[SPEAKER_01]: But the overall liability

616
01:02:39,567 --> 01:02:41,928
[SPEAKER_01]: is being increased as a result of you giving it.

617
01:02:42,188 --> 01:02:52,212
[SPEAKER_01]: So, next thing comes up, you get bored of whatever pleasant object that was, and you will find out, wait, actually they didn't solve the problem.

618
01:02:56,333 --> 01:02:59,735
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, boredom is also on the level of the hindrance or restlessness.

619
01:03:01,450 --> 01:03:02,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yeah, boredom.

620
01:03:02,330 --> 01:03:17,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what would happen if, I think I said that before, what would happen if you're thrown into solitary confinement after you've been indulging in sensuality and distractions for a whole day?

621
01:03:18,600 --> 01:03:24,442
[SPEAKER_01]: And what will... how will that compare to when you haven't, when you have been restrained?

622
01:03:25,142 --> 01:03:40,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, the boredom would hit you way, way harder, if you've... because it's exactly like abruptly cutting off the... like a train that's been moving in, I don't know, 200 miles per hour.

623
01:03:43,467 --> 01:03:49,094
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, but the boredom, whichever way you take it, whether it's restlessness or at least maybe boredom, the whole point is

624
01:04:00,179 --> 01:04:01,440
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to be liable to it.

625
01:04:01,580 --> 01:04:01,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

626
01:04:02,140 --> 01:04:03,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Precisely because of the sensuality.

627
01:04:04,180 --> 01:04:04,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

628
01:04:04,841 --> 01:04:07,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Even though you might see it as a completely separate thing.

629
01:04:07,742 --> 01:04:08,942
[SPEAKER_00]: You may not even see the connection.

630
01:04:09,242 --> 01:04:09,422
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

631
01:04:09,702 --> 01:04:11,843
[SPEAKER_02]: That was precisely what I was trying to get at.

632
01:04:11,863 --> 01:04:12,063
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

633
01:04:12,203 --> 01:04:20,406
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the whole idea that you can just be like hindered by one hindrance and which is unrelated to the other four, that's quite a wrong understanding of the hindrance.

634
01:04:28,686 --> 01:04:34,910
[SPEAKER_04]: I had a question, which is, I guess, in some ways related.

635
01:04:35,230 --> 01:04:36,210
[SPEAKER_04]: It was about contentment.

636
01:04:36,671 --> 01:04:42,034
[SPEAKER_04]: So, developing contentment would help to unhinder one.

637
01:04:42,915 --> 01:04:50,180
[SPEAKER_04]: And it's based off of this sutta where Sariputta is giving a discourse.

638
01:04:51,773 --> 01:04:58,154
[SPEAKER_04]: At first, going forth is difficult, and then contentment is difficult to develop, and so on, and so on.

639
01:04:59,174 --> 01:05:07,576
[SPEAKER_04]: And so, I was wondering, maybe, what this contentment that Sariputta is referring to, so maybe it could be elaborated on.

640
01:05:07,896 --> 01:05:18,398
[SPEAKER_04]: But, however, it's actually just what we've been discussing here, as well, about training the... training the mind, and...

641
01:05:21,428 --> 01:05:30,633
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, in some way, you're going to become careless when you're discontent with what's offered.

642
01:05:30,933 --> 01:05:32,294
[SPEAKER_04]: At least, that's how I can see it.

643
01:05:32,374 --> 01:05:36,997
[SPEAKER_04]: But I'm not sure if that's completely what's being referred to.

644
01:05:37,357 --> 01:05:41,279
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what would people think contentment is, in the right sense?

645
01:05:41,559 --> 01:05:47,063
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's... because, eh... some...

646
01:05:49,238 --> 01:05:54,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Marginal amount of contentment, people might think, comes from the world, in centrality.

647
01:05:54,906 --> 01:05:59,633
[SPEAKER_01]: But what would be the difference between the contentment that comes from rightly chaining the mind?

648
01:06:04,740 --> 01:06:05,621
[SPEAKER_01]: And what would be the difference?

649
01:06:17,174 --> 01:06:26,181
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I guess most obvious thing would be that the first would be dependent on... by definition, dependent on the circumstances.

650
01:06:27,042 --> 01:06:37,310
[SPEAKER_03]: So you'd recognize, oh, if this is taken away, or burns down, or dies, how would I be left with then?

651
01:06:37,950 --> 01:06:39,011
[SPEAKER_03]: Or how would that affect me?

652
01:06:39,572 --> 01:06:41,113
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas the other one would basically

653
01:06:43,223 --> 01:06:46,205
[SPEAKER_03]: We'll be completely independent of all of that, by definition.

654
01:06:46,685 --> 01:06:52,888
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's independent of external circumstances, of course.

655
01:06:53,228 --> 01:06:56,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody who is inclined to a spirituality would recognize it as a good thing.

656
01:06:57,791 --> 01:07:05,255
[SPEAKER_01]: But it has to ultimately be independent from internal circumstances as well, from deterioration of your own faculties.

657
01:07:06,004 --> 01:07:24,795
[SPEAKER_01]: From arising of pressure on a mental level, because that's another circumstance, independent of unpleasant feelings, independent of unpleasant moods, independent of whatever mental things can come up, regardless of your wish for them not to come up.

658
01:07:25,575 --> 01:07:32,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when contentment depends on having a certain feeling for that matter,

659
01:07:33,894 --> 01:07:38,555
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the right type of contentment, because pleasant feeling is impermanent, as the Buddha said.

660
01:07:38,615 --> 01:07:46,118
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh... the right type of contentment is when it doesn't even matter when you feel this pleasure.

661
01:07:47,298 --> 01:07:51,579
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only one that will survive everything, basically.

662
01:07:51,779 --> 01:07:59,522
[SPEAKER_01]: And the... eh... when... that's why it says that it's difficult to define contentment.

663
01:07:59,542 --> 01:08:00,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's... it's...

664
01:08:02,896 --> 01:08:10,422
[SPEAKER_05]: It's because the contentment that you have to find lies precisely in the direction that initially will be felt as discontent.

665
01:08:11,563 --> 01:08:14,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, eh... giving up, acting out of pressure.

666
01:08:15,927 --> 01:08:19,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Even though you've already gone forth, as a sutta says, like you already went forth.

667
01:08:20,831 --> 01:08:23,753
[SPEAKER_00]: But despite going forth, you still haven't arrived at contentment.

668
01:08:24,774 --> 01:08:33,481
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because sometimes you might think like, oh, if I just become a monk and just give up everything, I'll be happy with nothing, or something like that.

669
01:08:34,082 --> 01:08:46,512
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's way more subtle than that, because you still will have the tendency to internally act out of pressure and seek to seek the contentment on the level of managing your feelings.

670
01:08:46,592 --> 01:08:48,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, like doing things that

671
01:08:50,575 --> 01:08:51,316
[SPEAKER_01]: To get rid of pain.

672
01:08:52,316 --> 01:09:06,583
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as that remains your your hope of achieving contentment, fundamentally, externally, you might be more restrained, but internally, you still maintain the same principle, which, ironically, is what makes you discontent.

673
01:09:06,603 --> 01:09:16,328
[SPEAKER_01]: The need to have conditions be a certain way, even internally, that's what makes you discontent.

674
01:09:16,428 --> 01:09:19,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Because deep down, you know, it will deteriorate.

675
01:09:19,917 --> 01:09:21,678
[SPEAKER_01]: And deep down, you know that you have to protect it.

676
01:09:21,838 --> 01:09:39,591
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, compared to the actual contentment, it's not even... it's not even contentment, because it comes with the implicit worry and implicit kind of notice that it could be taken away at any time.

677
01:09:41,753 --> 01:09:47,157
[SPEAKER_01]: So, arriving at the right contentment will require, to some extent,

678
01:09:49,752 --> 01:09:56,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Enduring discontent, but that discontent, you have to see that it's not because of the restraint.

679
01:09:56,414 --> 01:09:59,415
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not because you're not giving in to the pressure that you're discontented.

680
01:10:01,175 --> 01:10:07,197
[SPEAKER_01]: You're discontented simply because you're resisting the pain of not giving in to the pressure.

681
01:10:07,357 --> 01:10:12,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you stop resisting the pain of not giving in to the pressure, that's exactly what the contentment is.

682
01:10:12,459 --> 01:10:16,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Not resisting... because we ask ourselves, why am I resisting?

683
01:10:18,709 --> 01:10:23,851
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize there's no justification for it.

684
01:10:24,672 --> 01:10:39,939
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you give that up, which, of course, sounds easier than it actually is, you realize, oh, yeah, I was discontent because I myself was making myself so, through my own wrong way of attending things, basically.

685
01:10:40,159 --> 01:10:45,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Still implying, to some extent, that I need to find a solution to this pain.

686
01:10:46,582 --> 01:10:47,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas,

687
01:10:47,981 --> 01:10:52,023
[SPEAKER_01]: The attitude of needing to find a solution is the reason why the pain is a problem in the first place.

688
01:10:54,684 --> 01:10:57,765
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, virtue would be purified?

689
01:10:58,666 --> 01:11:01,707
[SPEAKER_04]: Because there would be no way of you acting out?

690
01:11:01,867 --> 01:11:02,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, only then, yeah.

691
01:11:02,908 --> 01:11:09,390
[SPEAKER_04]: So, before that, you would be... Would it be like a pleasantness of a more virtuous life?

692
01:11:09,490 --> 01:11:12,672
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, still, to some extent, you would still be...

693
01:11:14,965 --> 01:11:17,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Valuing, certainly, the things of the world.

694
01:11:17,546 --> 01:11:24,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you would be giving them up because you hope that you will get something in return later on.

695
01:11:25,007 --> 01:11:27,868
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see directly here and now.

696
01:11:28,848 --> 01:11:34,329
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, the... like, sensuality takes time, but the dhamma is presently evident.

697
01:11:34,409 --> 01:11:40,871
[SPEAKER_01]: And you wouldn't see, in that presently evident sense, why giving these things up is already better.

698
01:11:42,159 --> 01:11:49,427
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be thinking like, oh, later on something will happen that will kind of make all of this worthwhile, or something like that, and that's another sensuality.

699
01:11:50,648 --> 01:12:02,000
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within the pain you have to finally escape, and that's when it will be independent of anything, any circumstance, any deterioration of conditions.

700
01:12:09,440 --> 01:12:10,621
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think then there's what it says.

701
01:12:11,421 --> 01:12:16,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you do find contentment, then it wouldn't take long.

702
01:12:17,363 --> 01:12:18,304
[SPEAKER_03]: There's one extra.

703
01:12:18,344 --> 01:12:19,044
[SPEAKER_03]: What's hard then?

704
01:12:19,784 --> 01:12:21,185
[SPEAKER_03]: Practicing in line with the teaching.

705
01:12:21,305 --> 01:12:21,525
[SPEAKER_03]: Right.

706
01:12:21,845 --> 01:12:22,145
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

707
01:12:22,365 --> 01:12:24,886
[SPEAKER_03]: But for someone who practices in line with the teaching, would it take long?

708
01:12:25,947 --> 01:12:26,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

709
01:12:27,892 --> 01:12:31,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that connects with what we've been saying before.

710
01:12:32,134 --> 01:12:34,595
[SPEAKER_01]: You might actually be contented, factually.

711
01:12:35,275 --> 01:12:37,036
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind has developed the clarity.

712
01:12:37,056 --> 01:12:42,198
[SPEAKER_01]: It sees things that go against the practice.

713
01:12:42,218 --> 01:12:53,123
[SPEAKER_01]: It sees them as unpleasant, as not worth it, as a charcoal pit, as the suttas would say.

714
01:12:54,232 --> 01:13:01,517
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet still, practicing the line of the dhamma isn't automatic.

715
01:13:01,597 --> 01:13:07,381
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a guarantee, it doesn't follow, that just because you're contented, you would automatically practice the line of the dhamma.

716
01:13:07,842 --> 01:13:13,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Which sounds kind of counterintuitive, but factually, that's how it is.

717
01:13:14,026 --> 01:13:19,050
[SPEAKER_01]: There's that sutta where it says, a monk might enter the first jhāna,

718
01:13:20,248 --> 01:13:24,689
[SPEAKER_01]: And somebody might think, oh, he will never go back to sensual pleasures.

719
01:13:25,389 --> 01:13:39,433
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha gives a simile, it's like when, you know, like when the... when it's been really dry, and then dust starts accumulating, and then it rains, and then somebody thinks, oh, this dust will never come back.

720
01:13:40,542 --> 01:13:43,164
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... and it goes all the way to the 4th jhāna.

721
01:13:44,045 --> 01:13:50,950
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... it's... it's... That is the factual, most accurate contentment that you could arrive at.

722
01:13:51,911 --> 01:13:57,876
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... and yet it says, but even then, after achieving that, somebody might even disrobe.

723
01:13:58,036 --> 01:14:01,739
[SPEAKER_01]: And the monk, and that's what the... that's exactly what he did.

724
01:14:02,640 --> 01:14:07,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, of course, that doesn't mean that, eh... that doesn't mean that the contentment

725
01:14:08,444 --> 01:14:11,946
[SPEAKER_01]: That we're referring to, like, factually, it has to be outside of sensuality.

726
01:14:13,627 --> 01:14:17,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Most of the time, what people would call jhāna, isn't actually outside of sensuality.

727
01:14:17,849 --> 01:14:19,850
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's even a deeper problem there.

728
01:14:19,910 --> 01:14:34,739
[SPEAKER_05]: But, even if you reach the real deal, yeah, the mind is still, in a way, precisely because of you having reached that greater perspective, you might disregard

729
01:14:35,881 --> 01:14:58,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else that you had to do in order to arrive at it, because it feels like, oh, well, the mind is, as Atsuta says, the elephant that's now invincible in battle, that can take everything, like swords, spears, and everything without reacting, and so you send it into battle, but it wasn't actually ready, and then it dies.

730
01:14:59,352 --> 01:14:59,672
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

731
01:15:05,159 --> 01:15:08,761
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there comes a point when you can't lose mindfulness?

732
01:15:11,082 --> 01:15:16,906
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, that point would come already, like... yeah, the point when you can't lose mindfulness is when you have samādhi.

733
01:15:18,227 --> 01:15:21,909
[SPEAKER_05]: That's... that factually is like, even if you want to, you can't lose it.

734
01:15:22,789 --> 01:15:32,635
[SPEAKER_05]: The problem is, if you, factually, without losing it, do certain things, you will suddenly find that you lost it.

735
01:15:33,779 --> 01:15:34,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's put it that way.

736
01:15:34,700 --> 01:15:39,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you were not losing it while you were doing those things, or at least it didn't feel like you were.

737
01:15:41,469 --> 01:15:44,393
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when you turn around, you realize, actually, it wasn't there.

738
01:15:45,414 --> 01:15:47,857
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how it works, because it's that indirect dimension.

739
01:15:48,458 --> 01:15:49,799
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that indirect kind of...

740
01:15:52,331 --> 01:15:54,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the kind of ultimate background of your experience.

741
01:15:54,893 --> 01:16:07,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you would start taking it for granted, basically, that you would start doing things thinking like, oh, well, yeah, the hindrances are gone, it doesn't matter.

742
01:16:08,061 --> 01:16:18,488
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's that gradual kind of inclining, as the Buddha would say, what you incline, what you frequently think and ponder about, that's what the mind inclines to.

743
01:16:19,649 --> 01:16:21,310
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a gradual kind of

744
01:16:23,380 --> 01:16:34,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Bit by bit, pushing it back in that direction, and then you will find, oh, actually it is... now it's more on that side than the right side that I had it in initially.

745
01:16:53,513 --> 01:17:09,264
[SPEAKER_01]: That's one thing that people would really struggle to understand when we say, like, the... in order to enter the first jhāna, you factually have to give up even the liability to the five hindrances, not just, like, specific manifestations.

746
01:17:09,284 --> 01:17:16,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then it would sound like, also, you're saying you have to basically become an arahant in order to enter the first jhāna.

747
01:17:16,829 --> 01:17:17,430
[SPEAKER_01]: But the thing is,

748
01:17:18,998 --> 01:17:23,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the liability to the hindrances is factually gone at that time, whether you like it or not.

749
01:17:24,641 --> 01:17:28,243
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean that they will not come back.

750
01:17:31,765 --> 01:17:44,853
[SPEAKER_05]: Because if you incline the mind, if you give in to the things that, at that time, don't feel like they're a problem, the hindrances will return.

751
01:17:48,757 --> 01:17:50,498
[SPEAKER_05]: Not immediately, but they will return.

752
01:17:51,358 --> 01:17:58,901
[SPEAKER_04]: And there's no removing the liability, to the liability, of the hindrances.

753
01:17:59,221 --> 01:18:02,263
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just that they are... staying there.

754
01:18:03,043 --> 01:18:12,407
[SPEAKER_05]: By staying there, by staying in the state where you're not liable to hindrances, then, then, yeah, you would... any inclination to

755
01:18:13,298 --> 01:18:15,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Turn the mind back towards the hindrances, it would be gone.

756
01:18:15,899 --> 01:18:21,820
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you wouldn't fall away from it out of the unwholesome reasons.

757
01:18:22,820 --> 01:18:27,461
[SPEAKER_01]: You would fall away from it just because of circumstantial reasons.

758
01:18:27,841 --> 01:18:39,184
[SPEAKER_01]: But not like someone who's not free from sensuality, who falls away because factually they start, like, the the fetters and stuff, get them to incline the mind in the opposite direction again.

